1
00:00:00,622 --> 00:00:03,716
If you called the last video, you already closed your DNS leak.

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:04,484
Good.

3
00:00:05,294 --> 00:00:06,660
Here's the uncomfortable part.

4
00:00:06,926 --> 00:00:15,492
Even with a clean DNS result and your VPN run-in, your browser may still be handing out your real IP address to every site you visit.

5
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:20,836
Not your provider this time, the actual address tied to your home.

6
00:00:21,294 --> 00:00:25,028
And for this one, your VPN often can't help you.

7
00:00:25,678 --> 00:00:34,880
In one well-known test of 100 VPNs by VoidSec, nearly one in five leaked the user's real IP through this exact hole.

8
00:00:35,086 --> 00:00:38,240
The feature responsible is called WebRTC.

9
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:40,740
It's built into every major browser.

10
00:00:40,974 --> 00:00:45,636
It runs silently and it bypasses your VPN by design.

11
00:00:46,382 --> 00:00:52,292
You can check it in 20 seconds, and I'll show you how to close it, even if your VPN won't.

12
00:00:52,814 --> 00:01:09,956
Now, in this video, we're gonna cover what WebRTC is and why it leaks, a live test, why your VPN can't fix it, the browser fixes and their catch, the architectural solutions, and the 30 second monthly checklist.

13
00:01:10,766 --> 00:01:13,200
Now, this is part two of the leak series.

14
00:01:13,390 --> 00:01:17,604
Full playlist is linked below, and don't forget subscribe for part three.

15
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,772
What WebRTC is and what a leak is.

16
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:22,720
Okay, quick foundation.

17
00:01:22,926 --> 00:01:23,840
WebRTC.

18
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:32,612
Web real-time communication is the technology that lets your browser run video calls and voice chat without installing anything extra.

19
00:01:32,878 --> 00:01:37,700
It's what powers Google Meet, Discord in the browser, that kind of thing.

20
00:01:38,126 --> 00:01:42,180
It's built into Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari, and Brave.

21
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:44,452
Billions of devices.

22
00:01:45,198 --> 00:01:52,356
But here's the problem to connect you directly to someone for a call, WebRTC has to know your real IP address.

23
00:01:52,814 --> 00:01:56,324
It finds it using something called a stun request.

24
00:01:56,800 --> 00:02:03,812
That request goes straight out through your network interface, around your VPN tunnel, not through it.

25
00:02:04,494 --> 00:02:07,204
So, a WebRTC leak is this.

26
00:02:07,854 --> 00:02:19,108
Any website using about 15 lines of JavaScript with no permission prompt can quietly ask your browser what your real IP address is.

27
00:02:19,534 --> 00:02:24,228
And your browser answers honestly, even with the VPN on.

28
00:02:24,814 --> 00:02:28,708
The feature that makes video calls work is the same one.

29
00:02:28,974 --> 00:02:32,100
Handing out your address to anyone who asks.

30
00:02:33,518 --> 00:02:34,276
The live test.

31
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:37,380
Alright, VPN connected?

32
00:02:38,030 --> 00:02:38,240
Good.

33
00:02:38,510 --> 00:02:40,068
Alright, slow down and follow along.

34
00:02:40,718 --> 00:02:44,228
Open browserleaks.com forward slash webRTC.

35
00:02:44,430 --> 00:02:46,000
The test runs automatically.

36
00:02:46,222 --> 00:02:49,188
Now you'll see two kinds of address local and public.

37
00:02:49,550 --> 00:02:56,132
Local ones starting with 192.168 or 10.R your home networks, harmless.

38
00:02:56,718 --> 00:02:59,120
The one that matters is the public IP.

39
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:04,100
Now if that public IP is your real one, not your VPNs, you're leaking.

40
00:03:04,782 --> 00:03:06,980
Same double check as last time.

41
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:10,468
Note your real IP with the VPN off.

42
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:12,452
Then reconnect and run it again.

43
00:03:12,718 --> 00:03:17,540
If the same address shows up while the VPN is on, the leak is confirmed.

44
00:03:18,318 --> 00:03:26,820
iPleak.net covers this alongside DNS and IPv6 if you want everything on one page.

45
00:03:27,342 --> 00:03:30,404
Now go ahead and drop your results in the comments.

46
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:32,004
Clean or leaked?

47
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:36,100
And if it's leaking, mention which browser you're using.

48
00:03:36,558 --> 00:03:44,880
Because this leak lives in the browser, not the network, and that information helps everyone else figure out which setups are actually safe.

49
00:03:45,678 --> 00:03:47,460
Why your VPN can't stop it.

50
00:03:48,238 --> 00:03:51,396
So why is this one different from the DNS leak?

51
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:56,708
Well because it doesn't happen at the network level, it happens inside your browser.

52
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:59,876
Your VPN encrypts traffic going through its tunnel.

53
00:04:00,206 --> 00:04:08,356
WebRTC's stun request doesn't use that tunnel, it goes out through a side door directly through your network interface.

54
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:13,444
A lot of VPNs never even see it, so they can't block it.

55
00:04:14,030 --> 00:04:18,308
That's not a bug in one bad product, that's the design.

56
00:04:19,182 --> 00:04:28,612
VoidSec tested 100 VPN providers and found 16 of them leaked the user's real IP through WebRTC, nearly one in five.

57
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:40,548
The top VPNs have since added browser extension patches, but those only work if you install them, keep them updated, and use them on every browser on every device.

58
00:04:41,166 --> 00:04:46,960
The fix has to happen in the browser, so let's go through exactly how to do that.

59
00:04:48,078 --> 00:04:49,124
The standard fixes.

60
00:04:50,144 --> 00:04:51,904
So, browser by browser.

61
00:04:51,984 --> 00:04:54,148
When we get to yours, go ahead and screenshot it.

62
00:04:54,704 --> 00:04:56,004
First up, Firefox.

63
00:04:56,224 --> 00:04:57,060
This is the easiest.

64
00:04:57,358 --> 00:05:00,100
Type about config in the address bar.

65
00:05:00,464 --> 00:05:07,332
Accept the warning, search media.peerconnection.enabled and set it to false.

66
00:05:07,982 --> 00:05:09,892
WebRTC is fully off.

67
00:05:10,286 --> 00:05:12,644
Works on Firefox for Android 2.

68
00:05:13,070 --> 00:05:14,500
Chrome and Edge.

69
00:05:14,784 --> 00:05:16,452
No built-in switch anymore.

70
00:05:16,782 --> 00:05:20,292
Google removed the old flag, so you need an extension.

71
00:05:20,654 --> 00:05:23,524
WebRTC Network Limiter is Google's own.

72
00:05:23,950 --> 00:05:27,204
WebRTC Control is another reliable option.

73
00:05:27,598 --> 00:05:30,020
Install one, enable it, and done.

74
00:05:30,544 --> 00:05:31,664
Next is Brave.

75
00:05:32,014 --> 00:05:33,664
It's mostly handled for you already.

76
00:05:33,870 --> 00:05:41,508
Go to settings, then privacy and set WebRTC IP handling to disable non-proxied UDP.

77
00:05:42,478 --> 00:05:43,424
Next up, Tor.

78
00:05:43,984 --> 00:05:44,784
Nothing to do.

79
00:05:44,944 --> 00:05:47,396
It disables WebRTC by default.

80
00:05:48,078 --> 00:05:51,076
Safari and iOS, the hard case.

81
00:05:51,504 --> 00:05:58,404
Apple forces every browser on iPhone onto WebKit and there's no reliable native off switch.

82
00:05:58,624 --> 00:06:04,132
If you need it gone on iOS, Brave is the most consistent option available.

83
00:06:04,704 --> 00:06:12,260
After any of these, rerun browserleaks.com forward slash web RTC to confirm your real IP has gone.

84
00:06:12,878 --> 00:06:14,436
Now, the catch.

85
00:06:14,958 --> 00:06:15,908
Two of them in fact.

86
00:06:16,494 --> 00:06:21,540
One, fully disabling WebRTC breaks every browser video call you have.

87
00:06:21,934 --> 00:06:24,036
No meat, no Discord in the tab.

88
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:28,228
Two, you just fix one browser on one device.

89
00:06:28,558 --> 00:06:35,172
A new browser, a new phone, an update that resets a setting, and you're exposed again.

90
00:06:35,822 --> 00:06:37,604
Same ceiling as last time.

91
00:06:37,902 --> 00:06:41,540
You're patching the seams, not sealing the container.

92
00:06:42,062 --> 00:06:43,812
Do you even need a VPN for this?

93
00:06:44,942 --> 00:06:48,676
Well here's the sharper version of the point from the last video.

94
00:06:49,070 --> 00:06:55,664
For a Web RTC leak, a VPN isn't just optional, it's often the wrong tool for the problem entirely.

95
00:06:55,886 --> 00:06:58,692
The leak goes around the tunnel by design.

96
00:06:59,054 --> 00:07:01,188
Paying for a VPN doesn't close it.

97
00:07:01,678 --> 00:07:08,324
You're left flipping browser switches that break your calls, one device at a time, hoping nothing resets.

98
00:07:09,006 --> 00:07:23,332
What actually fixes WebRTC without breaking your video calls, without redoing the work on every browser, is stopping the fight with the browser settings and using a browser that's sealed from the very start.

99
00:07:24,270 --> 00:07:25,636
The fix at the source.

100
00:07:25,934 --> 00:07:26,500
Go login.

101
00:07:26,990 --> 00:07:30,544
Now, same approach as last time, and the reason is the same.

102
00:07:30,704 --> 00:07:35,716
A VPN wraps a normal browser and hopes nothing leaks around the edges.

103
00:07:36,238 --> 00:07:40,864
Go login builds each browser profile as a fully isolated container.

104
00:07:41,134 --> 00:07:48,836
WebRTC is handled per profile, so it can only ever report the proxy's IP, never your real one.

105
00:07:49,390 --> 00:07:52,260
DNS goes through the built-in proxy.

106
00:07:52,544 --> 00:07:58,180
Time zone, language, geolocation, and fingerprint all match the proxy location.

107
00:07:58,638 --> 00:08:04,100
There's no side door for WebRTC to exit through because the whole profile is sealed.

108
00:08:04,494 --> 00:08:11,172
You're not disabling WebRTC, you're handling it cleanly so your calls still work.

109
00:08:11,790 --> 00:08:14,148
Setup is done in just two minutes.

110
00:08:14,766 --> 00:08:17,732
On the Go Login dashboard, create a new profile.

111
00:08:18,126 --> 00:08:20,224
Pick a proxy or paste your own.

112
00:08:20,558 --> 00:08:24,260
Paid plans include two gigabytes of proxy traffic per month.

113
00:08:25,102 --> 00:08:26,884
Then there's nothing else to configure.

114
00:08:27,278 --> 00:08:32,196
The team's position is that the automatic settings are already optimal.

115
00:08:32,622 --> 00:08:39,268
Go login matches the WebRTC output, DNS, time zone, language, and geolocation to your proxy IP.

116
00:08:39,630 --> 00:08:43,364
Go ahead and click run, then before anything else, verify.

117
00:08:44,046 --> 00:08:50,628
Go ahead and open browserleaks.com forward slash WebRTC or iphey.com inside the profile.

118
00:08:51,104 --> 00:08:52,292
Check everything.

119
00:08:52,750 --> 00:08:58,788
No real IP, WebRTC consistent, DNS clean, WebGL clean.

120
00:08:59,184 --> 00:09:02,624
Make that the habit before literally every single session.

121
00:09:02,990 --> 00:09:09,380
Now go login starts at $9 a month with a 7-day free trial and a free plan covering free profiles.

122
00:09:09,904 --> 00:09:11,684
Link is in the description.

123
00:09:13,166 --> 00:09:14,724
Who this actually matters to.

124
00:09:15,886 --> 00:09:19,620
Well, it's the same people as last time with one addition.

125
00:09:20,206 --> 00:09:28,964
Marketing agencies running multiple accounts on one platform, a web RTC leak can expose the same real IP behind all of them.

126
00:09:29,344 --> 00:09:33,412
And detection systems link the accounts and ban the batch.

127
00:09:33,742 --> 00:09:37,988
Affiliate marketers checking how campaigners appear from different regions.

128
00:09:38,446 --> 00:09:41,956
One leaked real IP and the data is worthless.

129
00:09:42,542 --> 00:09:43,556
Web scraping teams.

130
00:09:43,904 --> 00:09:51,108
This one is particularly relevant because antipod systems specifically watch for a real IP leaking through a proxy.

131
00:09:51,470 --> 00:09:54,660
WebRTC is one of the ways they catch it.

132
00:09:55,342 --> 00:10:03,172
Now different operations, same rule, the only IP anyone should see is the one that you choose to show them.

133
00:10:03,790 --> 00:10:09,956
A WebRTC leak breaks that quietly in the background on every site you visit.

134
00:10:10,510 --> 00:10:18,084
For anyone running an operation that depends on clean separation, testing is hygiene, not caution.

135
00:10:19,502 --> 00:10:20,452
The monthly checklist.

136
00:10:21,710 --> 00:10:23,744
Now the same point as last time.

137
00:10:23,918 --> 00:10:26,244
A clean result today doesn't stay clean.

138
00:10:26,574 --> 00:10:33,732
A browser update, a new install, a reset setting, and the leak comes back without announcing itself.

139
00:10:34,510 --> 00:10:37,092
30 seconds once a month, do this.

140
00:10:37,664 --> 00:10:43,652
Connect your setup, open browserleaks.com forward slash WebRTC and check the public IP.

141
00:10:44,064 --> 00:10:48,324
Confirm only your proxy IP shows, never your real one.

142
00:10:48,878 --> 00:10:54,308
Verify WebRTC is handled in every browser you actually use.

143
00:10:55,022 --> 00:10:58,852
Recheck after any update or new browser installation.

144
00:10:59,264 --> 00:11:00,676
And that's it.

145
00:11:01,326 --> 00:11:05,796
Now you've now closed your DNS leak and your WebRTC leak.

146
00:11:06,144 --> 00:11:09,988
Two of the main routes your real identity uses to get out.

147
00:11:10,542 --> 00:11:12,004
There's one more piece.

148
00:11:12,590 --> 00:11:18,532
Every other way your real IP can surface in one place with one test that catches all of them.

149
00:11:19,054 --> 00:11:20,420
That's the IP leak.

150
00:11:20,942 --> 00:11:26,564
The final part of this playlist and the one that ties everything together, check it out next.

151
00:11:27,150 --> 00:11:29,860
Run your tests and subscribe to stay ahead of it.

